/** \file p4_optimizer.dox
 * \brief User's guide for P4 negative-planet optimization.
 * \author Jared R. Males
 */

/** \addtogroup p4_optimizer_user_guide
 * @{
 *
 * Negative-planet optimization repeatedly calls the exact finite-amplitude
 * \ref p4_local_user_guide "pixel-local P4 path" against one pristine, already-loaded target cube. It can fit signed
 * negative contrast at a fixed location or jointly fit contrast and position. Intermediate evaluations remain in
 * memory and do not write ordinary P4 products.
 *
 * \section p4_optimizer_algorithm Optimization and merit
 *
 * With `p4Optimize.fitPosition=false`, separation and position angle remain fixed at the single configured `[fake]`
 * tuple while a deterministic bounded Brent search minimizes contrast. With `fitPosition=true`, that contrast result
 * seeds a bounded Nelder-Mead search in Cartesian row offset, column offset, and contrast. A second fixed-position
 * dense+Brent contrast fit validates the final photometry.
 *
 * Cartesian offsets preserve the `0.5*(N-1)` image-center convention and avoid position-angle wrap internally. The
 * merit is the selected P4 plane's uniform mean-square residual within a circular aperture centered on the initial
 * continuous sky coordinate:
 *
 * \f[
 * J(\theta)=\frac{\sum_i v_i w_i R(\theta,i)^2}{\sum_i v_i w_i}.
 * \f]
 *
 * The current weights \f$w_i\f$ are uniform and every required validity value \f$v_i\f$ must be nonzero. Joint
 * trials move the injected source but retain exactly the same full-image pixels in the aperture. This fixed sky
 * aperture prevents the optimizer from improving its score by moving a noisy boundary pixel into or out of the
 * measurement.
 *
 * \section p4_optimizer_configuration Configuration
 *
 * The complete \ref p4_local_user_guide "pixel-local configuration contract" applies. The initial source and
 * template come from the single `[fake]` tuple.
 *
 * | CLI | Config key/value shorthand | Best-product FITS keyword | Type and limits | Default | Meaning |
 * |---|---|---|---|---|---|
 * | `--p4Optimize.enabled` | `p4Optimize.enabled=true` | `HIERARCH P4 OPTIMIZER` | bool | `false` | Run negative-planet optimization instead of one ordinary reduction. |
 * | `--p4Optimize.modeFraction` | `p4Optimize.modeFraction=0.15` | `HIERARCH P4 OPT MODE FRACTION` | exact member of `p4.modeFractions` | `0.15` | Single output plane used by the scalar merit. A nearest plane is never selected silently. |
 * | `--p4Optimize.apertureRadius` | `p4Optimize.apertureRadius=5` | `HIERARCH P4 OPT APERTURE RADIUS` | nonnegative real pixels | `0` | Fixed-sky uniform-L2 aperture radius. Zero selects `2*p4.psfRadius`. Joint mode requires a local-stamp half-width of at least `apertureRadius+positionBound`. |
 * | `--p4Optimize.fitPosition` | `p4Optimize.fitPosition=true` | `HIERARCH P4 OPT FIT POSITION` | bool | `false` | Jointly fit Cartesian astrometry and contrast. |
 * | `--p4Optimize.contrastLower` | `p4Optimize.contrastLower=-0.05` | `HIERARCH P4 OPT CONTRAST LOWER` | finite real | `-0.05` | Inclusive signed lower contrast bound. |
 * | `--p4Optimize.contrastUpper` | `p4Optimize.contrastUpper=0` | `HIERARCH P4 OPT CONTRAST UPPER` | finite real, greater than lower and at most zero | `0` | Inclusive signed upper bound; the initial `fake.contrast` must lie within both bounds. |
 * | `--p4Optimize.maxEvaluations` | `p4Optimize.maxEvaluations=192` | `HIERARCH P4 OPT EVALUATIONS` | integer at least `validationSamples+3`; joint minimum `2*(validationSamples+16)+4` | `64` | Hard limit on distinct local P4 calls. Joint mode reserves two complete contrast stages; 192 is recommended with 21 validation samples. |
 * | `--p4Optimize.validationSamples` | `p4Optimize.validationSamples=21` | — | integer at least 3 | `21` | Uniform dense-grid size, including both contrast bounds. |
 * | `--p4Optimize.parameterTolerance` | `p4Optimize.parameterTolerance=1e-5` | `HIERARCH P4 OPT CONTRAST TOLERANCE` | positive finite real | `1e-5` | Absolute contrast-width tolerance. |
 * | `--p4Optimize.positionTolerance` | `p4Optimize.positionTolerance=0.001` | `HIERARCH P4 OPT POSITION TOLERANCE` | positive finite real pixels | `0.001` | Cartesian simplex-diameter tolerance used by joint fitting. |
 * | `--p4Optimize.meritTolerance` | `p4Optimize.meritTolerance=1e-6` | `HIERARCH P4 OPT MERIT TOLERANCE` | nonnegative finite real | `1e-6` | Combined absolute/relative allowance, scaled by `max(1,abs(merit))`, for simplex convergence and dense-basin agreement. |
 * | `--p4Optimize.positionBound` | `p4Optimize.positionBound=1` | `HIERARCH P4 OPT POSITION BOUND` | positive finite real pixels in joint mode | `1` | Independent symmetric row/column bounds around the initial source. |
 * | `--p4Optimize.uncertaintyBlocks` | `p4Optimize.uncertaintyBlocks=8` | `HIERARCH P4 OPT JK BLOCKS` | zero or integer at least 2 | `8` | Contiguous delete-one-time-block refit count. Zero disables uncertainty; use at least eight blocks for a reported science result. |
 * | `--p4Optimize.outputPrefix` | `p4Optimize.outputPrefix=p4Negative_` | — | nonempty relative prefix | `p4Negative_` | Product prefix inside the resolved final image's `_outputs` directory; parent directories are created automatically. |
 *
 * A contrast-only configuration for an 11-pixel local stamp is:
 *
 * \code{.ini}
 * [p4]
 * localStampSize=11
 *
 * [p4Optimize]
 * enabled=true
 * modeFraction=0.15
 * apertureRadius=5
 * contrastLower=-0.05
 * contrastUpper=0
 * maxEvaluations=64
 * validationSamples=21
 * parameterTolerance=1e-5
 * meritTolerance=1e-6
 * outputPrefix=p4Negative_
 * \endcode
 *
 * The existing `fake.sep` and `fake.PA` remain fixed. Set `fake.contrast` to a finite initial value within the
 * configured bounds; the required dense scan supplies the numerical starting basin.
 *
 * To fit astrometry as well, retain that initial `[fake]` tuple and use:
 *
 * \code{.ini}
 * [p4]
 * localStampSize=13
 *
 * [p4Optimize]
 * enabled=true
 * fitPosition=true
 * positionBound=1
 * modeFraction=0.15
 * apertureRadius=5
 * contrastLower=-0.05
 * contrastUpper=0
 * maxEvaluations=192
 * validationSamples=21
 * parameterTolerance=1e-5
 * positionTolerance=0.001
 * meritTolerance=1e-6
 * outputPrefix=p4NegativeFull_
 * \endcode
 *
 * A radius-5 aperture and one-pixel position search require `p4.localStampSize=13` or larger. An 11-pixel stamp is
 * sufficient for contrast-only fitting because neither its source nor its aperture moves.
 *
 * \section p4_optimizer_jackknife Jackknife uncertainty
 *
 * After a converged complete-sequence fit, the optimizer partitions the loaded input frames into \f$B\f$ contiguous,
 * nearly equal time blocks. These are blocks of image planes in acquisition order, after any configured input
 * preparation or coadding; they are not blocks of pixels. The same local stamp, optimization pixels, and fixed merit
 * aperture are used in every refit.
 *
 * The optimizer then repeats the complete fit \f$B\f$ times. Refit \f$b\f$ omits every frame in time block \f$b\f$
 * from both the central-target samples and the eligible temporal-predictor pool. With `p4.numberImages` enabled,
 * neighboring predictors are reselected from the retained frames. Thus each refit asks how much the inferred
 * companion changes when one contiguous part of the observing sequence is absent.
 * Contiguous blocks retain some sensitivity to time-correlated speckle evolution that would be hidden by deleting
 * isolated frames, without assuming that individual image pixels are independent measurements.
 *
 * Each refit produces a parameter estimate \f$\theta_b\f$. For a joint position-and-contrast fit this is the vector
 *
 * \f[
 * \theta_b =
 * \left(
 * \begin{array}{c}
 * \Delta r_b \\
 * \Delta c_b \\
 * C_b
 * \end{array}
 * \right),
 * \f]
 *
 * where \f$\Delta r_b\f$ and \f$\Delta c_b\f$ are Cartesian row and column displacements from the originally
 * configured source position and \f$C_b\f$ is signed contrast. For a contrast-only fit, \f$\theta_b=C_b\f$ is a
 * scalar. Using Cartesian displacements avoids position-angle wrap in the covariance. For the arithmetic mean
 * \f$\bar{\theta}=B^{-1}\sum_b\theta_b\f$, the reported delete-one-block jackknife covariance is
 *
 * \f[
 * {\rm Cov}_{\mathrm{JK}}(\theta)
 * = \frac{B-1}{B}\sum_{b=1}^{B}
 * (\theta_b-\bar{\theta})(\theta_b-\bar{\theta})^{\mathsf T}.
 * \f]
 *
 * The covariance parameter order is row displacement, column displacement, and contrast. Standard errors are the
 * square roots of its diagonal, while the off-diagonal terms preserve fitted astrometry/photometry correlations for
 * downstream analysis. In contrast-only mode only the contrast variance and standard error are scientifically
 * meaningful.
 *
 * The reported point estimate always comes from the fit to the complete input sequence. The jackknife mean is a
 * diagnostic of the refit ensemble, not a replacement point estimate. Joint jackknife searches are centered on the
 * converged complete-sequence fit, but their tabulated displacements remain referenced to the originally configured
 * source position. Changing that common coordinate origin would not change the covariance.
 *
 * `p4Optimize.maxEvaluations` applies independently to the point fit and to every refit, so uncertainty can cost
 * approximately \f$B\f$ times the point-fit runtime. The default eight blocks implement the intended scientific
 * baseline. Values of two or more support development and sensitivity checks, while zero explicitly disables the
 * stage. A block count greater than the loaded frame count is rejected. Because this application of a time-block
 * jackknife is still experimental, compare results across reasonable block counts: blocks should be long enough to
 * represent temporal correlations but numerous enough to estimate a stable covariance. These uncertainties measure
 * sensitivity to temporal data selection; they do not include PSF-template calibration, plate scale, true-north, or
 * other external systematic errors.
 *
 * \section p4_optimizer_products Products and convergence
 *
 * All optimizer products are written below the resolved final image's auxiliary directory using
 * `p4Optimize.outputPrefix`. For example, an ordinary final-image name `finim_0044.fits` uses
 * `finim_0044_outputs/`; an exact configured name `science.fits` uses `science_outputs/`:
 * An optimizer-only run therefore reserves its sequential final-image number even though it does not write the
 * corresponding top-level `finim_NNNN.fits`. Later reductions choose one greater than the highest sequence occupied
 * by either a final FITS file or an `_outputs` directory, so deleting an older product does not cause its number to
 * be reused.
 *
 * - **`<prefix>summary.yaml`** is the compact machine-readable result summary. It records the initial and fitted
 *   separation, position angle, and signed contrast; Cartesian displacement; merit and aperture definition;
 *   optimizer bounds, tolerances, convergence flags, evaluation counts, and timing. Its `jackknife` mapping records
 *   completion and cost. When every refit succeeds it also contains the jackknife mean, standard errors, and full
 *   covariance matrix with explicit parameter order. Use the `fitted` mapping as the complete-sequence point
 *   estimate and the jackknife `standardError` and `covariance` fields as its internal statistical uncertainty.
 *
 * - **`<prefix>merit.csv`** contains one row per distinct complete-sequence optimizer evaluation. Contrast-only rows
 *   contain `evaluation,stage,contrast,merit,elapsed_seconds`. Joint rows additionally contain Cartesian displacement,
 *   separation, and position angle. Joint stages are `seed-dense`, `seed-brent`, `simplex`, `final-dense`, and
 *   `final-brent`; contrast-only stages are `dense` and `brent`. Use this table to inspect the merit surface, verify
 *   that the optimum is not pinned to a configured bound, identify competing minima, and diagnose expensive trials.
 *   It is optimization history, not an uncertainty sample.
 *
 * - **`<prefix>best.fits`** is the local residual image cube evaluated at the complete-sequence best-fit parameters.
 *   Its spatial dimensions are `p4.localStampSize` by `p4.localStampSize`, and its planes follow the configured
 *   `p4.modeFractions` order. The FITS header records the optimizer configuration and result, selected merit plane,
 *   aperture geometry, convergence state, jackknife statistics, and `HIERARCH P4 PRODUCT ROLE=LOCAL_RESIDUAL`. Use it
 *   to inspect whether the companion signal and surrounding structured residual are actually removed; do not use its
 *   pixel scatter as a substitute for the jackknife parameter covariance.
 *
 * - **`<prefix>best_validity.fits`** is the zero/one support cube corresponding exactly to `<prefix>best.fits`, with
 *   `HIERARCH P4 PRODUCT ROLE=LOCAL_VALIDITY`. A zero marks an unsupported residual pixel. Apply this cube as a mask
 *   whenever measuring or displaying the residual product; it describes algorithmic support, not confidence or
 *   statistical weight.
 *
 * - **`<prefix>jackknife.csv`** is written when `p4Optimize.uncertaintyBlocks` is nonzero. It has one row per omitted
 *   time block, including the zero-based block number, the zero-based half-open frame interval `[omitted_begin,
 *   omitted_end)`, retained frame count, convergence and dense-agreement flags, fitted Cartesian and polar
 *   parameters, merit, evaluation count, elapsed time, and status. Row and column displacements use the original
 *   configured source as their common origin. Inspect this table for a single influential observing interval,
 *   boundary-pinned solutions, or nonconverged refits before accepting the aggregate errors. The rows are correlated
 *   leave-one-block-out estimates and should not be treated as independent companion measurements.
 *
 * - **`<prefix>best.conf`** is a reusable INI fragment containing the fitted `[fake]` separation, position angle, and
 *   signed negative contrast. It is written only when the complete-sequence fit converges, agrees with dense
 *   validation, and every requested jackknife refit converges. It can be included in a subsequent signal-subtracted
 *   reduction. If the PSF template is photometrically calibrated, the corresponding positive companion contrast is
 *   normally the negative of this fitted signed contrast; calibration systematics remain external to the optimizer.
 *
 * A practical acceptance sequence is therefore: require complete-sequence convergence and dense agreement; require
 * jackknife completion when uncertainty was requested; inspect `merit.csv` for bounds or secondary basins; inspect
 * every row of `jackknife.csv` for instability; then examine `best.fits` through the `best_validity.fits` mask. Use the
 * complete-sequence fitted parameters as the measurement and propagate the full jackknife covariance when position
 * and contrast are fitted jointly.
 *
 * An exhausted or dense-disagreeing complete-sequence search retains its best finite FITS evaluation and diagnostic
 * tables with an explicit failure status, does not write `<prefix>best.conf`, and returns failure from `p4Reduce`. A
 * requested jackknife with any unconverged block similarly retains the point-fit products and resample table, marks
 * uncertainty incomplete, removes any stale `<prefix>best.conf` from an earlier run with the same prefix, and returns
 * failure. Missing covariance fields in the YAML/FITS products therefore mean that no complete jackknife uncertainty
 * estimate was produced; they must not be interpreted as zero uncertainty.
 *
 * @}
 */
